Subject: [PATCH] em: a deleted node is freed only after its thread ended and the nodes listener let it go

em_mgr_t::delete_node (RemoveDevice's em_delete) and delete_nodes (Reset's em_reset) freed a
node's em while its detached thread could still run: stop() set m_exit and signalled without
the queue's lock, deinit() destroyed the queue's lock and condition, and the em was deleted,
with nothing waiting for the thread to leave proto_run. The nodes listener could also still
hold the em, found in the map before it was removed, and push a frame into the freed queue.

- A node's thread is joinable. stop() sets m_exit and signals under the queue's lock, so the
  wake-up cannot fall between proto_run's check and its wait.
- delete_node and delete_nodes take the em out of the map under m_mutex and out of the
  orchestrator's commands first, then free_nodes stops each one, joins its thread (it ends
  once it handled what was queued to it; one EM_NODE_JOIN_MS deadline for them all), waits for
  the next pass of the nodes listener when it runs, then deinit and delete. An em whose thread
  does not end in time, or all of them when no listener pass comes, is detached and kept, not
  freed, and logged.
- The nodes listener takes the AL interface ems to read from the map under m_mutex and handles
  their frames without it: find_em_for_msg_type takes m_autoconfig_mutex and the liveness lock
  and can create a node, and em_ctrl_t takes those before the map's lock (the map's lock is
  innermost), so holding it across them could deadlock. Instead the listener marks a pass at
  the top of each loop, where it holds no em it found in the map.
- retire_node detaches the retired em's thread: the em stays allocated, as before, so its
  thread ends on its own.
- deinit frees what was still queued, and closes the descriptor only of an AL interface em,
  the only kind that opens one (a radio em's m_fd is 0, so this closed stdin).
- em_ctrl_t's and em_agent_t's destructors stop and free their nodes (stop_nodes) before their
  members go, so no node's thread calls back into a destroyed manager; m_em_map and m_orch start
  NULL, which they check.

Unit tests:
- The 18 em_ctrl_t_Test tests 0279 excluded for this race are run again and pass.
- delete_node_while_its_thread_is_busy: fifty rounds of a node with two hundred frames queued,
  deleted at once.
- delete_node_waits_for_a_listener_pass: with the listener running, delete_node frees the node
  only after its next pass; with none it waits for none.
- The orchestrator fixtures that destroy the map they made set m_em_map to NULL after it.
